def P(num,h,fig,tier,grant,inv,title,teaches,figs,status="Expired",**k):
    d=dict(num=num,hash=h,fig=fig,tier=tier,grant=grant,inv=inv,title=title,teaches=teaches,figs=figs,status=status); d.update(k); return d
INTRO = "<p>Fencing, bees, poultry and dairy gear were some of the most patented farm tools of the 1800s. These drawings show barbed wire, the movable-frame beehive and bellows smoker, a gravity poultry waterer and a dash churn anyone can make, then brooders and an incubator for raising chicks without a hen, and finally the centrifugal cream separators made in factories. Use them with the poultry, bees and dairy chapters of this guide.</p>"
UNPATENTED = ["The <b>Babcock butterfat test</b> (Stephen M. Babcock, University of Wisconsin, 1890) was deliberately <b>not patented</b>, so it stayed cheap and anyone could build and use it (UW&ndash;Madison Research, <i>Research Highlights 1848&ndash;1899</i>; saved in this guide's Patents folder).",
 "Rail, stone and hedge fences, hand milking, and the hen-and-nest-box method of hatching are ancient and free to copy."]
PATENTS = [
P("US157124","48/a8/0c/b40192435c3da4/US157124.pdf","2c/55/5c/a19dadfbcdf4e7/US157124-drawings-page-1.png","A","1874-11-24","Joseph F. Glidden","Improvement in wire-fences (barbed wire)",
  "Glidden's barbed wire: a short cross wire coiled round one strand of a two-strand twisted fence wire, with both ends sticking out as barbs; twisting the second strand round the first locks the barbs in place. It keeps cattle from pushing through a cheap wire fence. It can be made by hand with pliers and a crank twister.","Fig. 1 side view of fence section; Figs. 2&ndash;3 the coiled barb and the twisted strands"),
P("US9300","71/b8/4e/0122ed14d4ec59/US9300.pdf","ab/58/42/4dc2e7e4e28eca/US9300-drawings-page-2.png","A","1852-10-05","Lorenzo L. Langstroth","Beehive (movable-frame hive)",
  "Langstroth's movable-frame hive: comb is built in separate wooden frames hung inside a box, so each frame can be lifted out to inspect brood, take honey, or split a colony without breaking comb. It is still the standard hive design and can be built with hand tools from boards.","Box, hanging frames and cover sections"),
P("USRE8326","1a/0c/99/f19437d5a626df/USRE8326.pdf","22/28/e7/0c90069aa509d6/USRE8326-drawings-page-1.png","A","1878-07-09","Tracy F. Bingham","Bee-smoker (device for destroying insects by fumigation), reissue",
  "Bingham's bellows bee smoker: a small cylindrical fire can with a perforated fire grate and tapered nozzle, set on top of a hand bellows that blows air in underneath to push cool smoke out over the bees. This is the pattern every modern smoker copies, and it can be made from a tin can, leather and two boards.","Smoker in section: fire can, grate, nozzle and bellows"),
P("US798493","ac/40/b9/a0b90649b6e096/US798493.pdf","25/62/7b/cf29596bed6d4d/US798493-drawings-page-1.png","A","1905-08-29","Nicholas H. Brown","Poultry-fountain",
  "A gravity poultry waterer: a closed water tank stands upside-down in a shallow pan and refills it automatically as birds drink, keeping a steady low water level (the same air-lock principle as an upturned jar in a saucer). Clean water cuts disease in a flock.","Section through tank and drinking pan"),
P("US254121","30/e9/2a/7a504a32a30d57/US254121.pdf","d2/3d/12/fe3875f85259f1/US254121-drawings-page-1.png","A","1882-02-28","Henry T. Davis","Reciprocating churn",
  "Fits the ordinary up-and-down dash churn with a perforated inner shell and side tubes that carry hot or cold water, so cream can be warmed or cooled to churning temperature without being diluted.","Fig. 1 vertical section of the churn; Fig. 2 the shell and tubes; Fig. 3 the perforated disk"),
P("US331096","bd/b8/91/152632301c4c7c/US331096.pdf","20/d3/b4/89fac82e7b6d39/US331096-drawings-page-1.png","B","1885-11-24","Maurice H. Strong","Chicken-brooder",
  "A brooder (artificial mother) for chicks from a hen or an incubator: a warm, ventilated box with hanging flannel strips the chicks huddle under, and a floor that can be raised as they grow.","Fig. 1 long section; Fig. 2 brooder with casing removed and flannel strips"),
P("US656273","ac/2e/73/bbefaa14caf76d/US656273.pdf","60/45/cc/a73a5fb24ce2ff/US656273-drawings-page-1.png","B","1900-08-21","Jesse S. Stewart","Incubator",
  "A simple, cheap incubator. The egg chamber can be moved nearer to or farther from the heat source (closer in cold weather, farther in warm) to hold an even temperature, and it has ventilation for the eggs.","Sections through heater, egg chamber and ventilation"),
P("US732886","fb/bd/52/ac8b335dd186d2/US732886.pdf","c8/51/06/01cb6aad05f495/US732886-drawings-page-1.png","C","1903-07-07","Willard A. Odell &amp; Thomas F. Egan","Cream-separator",
  "A centrifugal cream separator. Milk spun in a fast bowl throws the heavier skim milk outward and the lighter cream gathers at the centre, separating far more completely than skimming a settled pan. The precision bowl and gearing need a machine shop.","Section through the bowl, inner separating parts and outlets"),
P("US715001","58/75/cb/b0125e692038a4/US715001.pdf","e5/87/ae/16215c8e65c834/US715001-drawings-page-1.png","C","1902-12-02","John Joseph Berrigan (assignor to the De Laval Separator Co.)","Centrifugal liquid-separator",
  "A De Laval-company centrifugal separator bowl: it shows how factory separators route milk through the spinning bowl and draw cream and skim off separately. It is a reference for repairing or understanding an old separator, not for building one from scratch.","Vertical section of the separator bowl"),
P("US1257634","5e/a8/cc/05a56286698407/US1257634.pdf","5c/2d/bc/e86477898df6a4/US1257634-drawings-page-1.png","A","1918-02-26","Albert M. Perrin","Animal-snare",
  "A hand-held catcher for hogs, used to catch and hold a pig by the snout or leg. A wire noose at the end of a handle stays tight as long as the animal pulls against it. A spring holds the noose open, so relaxing the grip on the handle releases the animal at once. It has few parts and is built to stand rough use.","Fig. 1 side view of the catcher with the noose open"),
P("US263228","d3/40/24/429fd6d5ef9ff4/US263228.pdf","82/40/bb/5129e199d567a3/US263228-drawings-page-1.png","B","1882-08-22","Joseph K. Rudyard","Centrifugal honey-extractor",
  "A centrifugal honey extractor. Wire-netting comb holders stand on a plate around a vertical shaft inside a vessel. Turning the shaft spins the combs, and centrifugal force throws the honey out of them. The combs can then be reversed to empty the other side without stopping the machine: gears under the holders turn them when the shaft is slowed. Read with Part 5, Honey Bees.","Fig. 1 section through the vessel, shaft and comb holders; Fig. 2 plan view"),
]
